n\nTherefore, my priorities:  \n1. Check if I have any direct five-in-a-row possibility elsewhere.  \n2. Block White’s open-three diagonal threat.  \n3. Create my own new threat (like an open-three or four).  \n\nWhite’s diagonal J7, K6, L5 can be extended to either I6 or M4 to make a four. Blocking at I6 or M4 is sensible. But I can also create a strong attack for myself.  \n\nLooking at Black’s stones, the cluster at row 8 suggests that placing X at B8 would connect to C8 and create a horizontal two (B8–C8) and could later build towards the left side.  \nAlternatively, placing X at J8 would connect to I8(O) but maybe not useful.  \n\nBut better: what about defending against the diagonal threat while setting up my own four?  \nThe most urgent defensive move: blocking at I6 or M4.  \n\nLet’s analyze winning move for Black: If I put X at D9, that doesn’t create five.  \nIf I put X at B8, no immediate five.  \nBut perhaps there’s a vertical/diagonal chance? Not evident.  \n\nGiven the board’s state, the only immediate winning possibility would be if I had an open four, which I don’t.  \nBut wait — what about **C8, E8, F8, G8, H8** plus placing X at D8? That’s not possible, D8 is O.  \n\nAfter re-checking, Black’s only hope for a fast win is to build a new four somewhere.  \n\nBut let’s verify the rule: Gomoku win is exactly five horizontally, vertically, or diagonally.  \nSo, can I win in one move? No, because no four with an empty adjacent spot exists.  \nBut there is one exception: **more than four in a row** still wins if five contiguous. So if I already have E8, F8, G8, H8, that’s four. I need one more connected to either end, but ends blocked. So no.  \n\nGiven this, I must defend and build.  \n\nBest defensive and offensive move: Block at **M4**, because:  \n- It blocks White’s diagonal threat (J7, K6, L5, next would be M4).  \n- It also starts a new diagonal for Black: M4, L5(O), K6(O), J7(O) — not good for me.  \nBut maybe better: Block at **I6**. Then White’s